🎓 Lab 2 Summary (as a student)

In this lab, I built a serverless web application on AWS without using any servers like EC2.

The idea is:

The frontend (index.html) is hosted on S3 (static website)
When the user interacts with the page, requests go to API Gateway
API Gateway triggers a Lambda function (Python)
Lambda processes the request and returns a response
🧱 What I built
S3 Website Bucket
Hosts the website (index.html)
Public so anyone can open it in the browser
S3 Data Bucket
Private bucket for file uploads
Not accessible directly (for security)
Lambda Function
/hello → returns a message like "Hello, Afaf!"
/presign → generates a presigned URL for uploading files
API Gateway
Connects the frontend with Lambda
Has routes:
/hello
/presign
🔁 How the flow works
User opens the website from S3
Clicks "Call API" → request goes to /hello → Lambda returns JSON
For upload:
Frontend calls /presign
Lambda returns a temporary upload URL
Browser uploads the file directly to S3
🧠 Key concept I learned

Lambda does NOT upload files.
It only gives permission (presigned URL), and the browser uploads directly to S3.

This makes the system:

Faster 🚀
Cheaper 💰
Scalable 🔥
⚠️ Common issues I faced
Failed to fetch → CORS مشكلة
No presigned URL → wrong response key
Upload failed → bucket name or permissions issue
✅ Final result
Website opens without AWS login
API works and returns data
File upload works
Files are stored securely in a private S3 bucket
💡 Final thought (as a student)

This lab helped me understand how to build a full backend using AWS services without managing servers, and how services like S3, Lambda, and API Gateway work together.
